  "body": {
    "id": "prop:equality-frontier",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For either constant comparator, relative to the corrected aggregate score-family optimum,\n\\[\n\\Delta(b)=E_P[(b-b^\\star(X))'\\Gamma(X)(b-b^\\star(X))].\n\\]\nMoreover \\(\\Delta(b)=0\\) if and only if \\(N'\\Gamma(X)b=0\\) almost surely.  Thus the pointwise residual exactly characterizes equality with the best aggregate conditional loading.  It characterizes equality with the full semiparametric bound only when the aggregate comparison closure exhausts the period-specific tangent normal space.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "bOVS",
      "bconst",
      "Deltafun",
      "P",
      "bstar",
      "X",
      "Gamma",
      "N",
      "dcmp",
      "Cclosure",
      "Tang"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:projection-diagnostic",
      "lem:singular-operator",
      "thm:complete-normal-canonical-gradient",
      "ass:contrast-spectral-gap"
    ],
    "justification": "The pointwise equations give a falsifiable equality frontier rather than a generic claim that conditional weighting helps.",
    "gap": "The banked constant-loading parent derives only an unconditional normal equation, and Ortiz-Villavicencio and Sant'Anna (2025) do not characterize equality with the conditional efficiency bound.",
    "consumer": "An empirical diagnostic can determine whether the published package backend already attains the bound at the fitted law.",
    "proof_tex": "Both \\(b\\) and \\(b^\\star(X)\\) are feasible.  Their corrected scores therefore have the same conditional mean \\(\\omega'h(X)\\).  The variance decomposition in the narrowed canonical-gradient result reduces their variance difference to the difference of conditional quadratic forms.  With \\(d=b-b^\\star=Nz\\), the pointwise normal equation gives\n\\[\nb'\\Gamma b-b^{\\star\\prime}\\Gamma b^\\star\n=2d'\\Gamma b^\\star+d'\\Gamma d=d'\\Gamma d=z'Hz.\n\\]\nIntegration proves the identity.  Also\n\\[\nN'\\Gamma b=N'\\Gamma(b-b^\\star)=Hz.\n\\]\nIf the residual is zero then \\(z'Hz=0\\), so the gap is zero.  Conversely, a zero expected gap and nonnegativity imply \\(z'H(X)z=0\\) almost surely.  Positive semidefiniteness then gives \\(H(X)z=0\\), hence the residual is zero almost surely.  Applying this argument to the Moore--Penrose definitions of \\(b^{\\mathrm{OVS}}\\) and \\(b^{\\mathrm{const}}\\) proves both comparator characterizations.  The final qualification follows from the strict tangent-space inclusion already proved.",
